nt to inclusion  in our society the demographics are  driving us in that direction I think  we've got to be sure that all kids who  are considered are meritorious but the  notion that you only pick based on  quantitative factors like test scores  and grades is wrongheaded you write the  following you say I know that America  hasn't gotten rid of racism and  discrimination everywhere in the land I  know that many public schools don't  educate minority children nearly as well  as they should but I beg you please  don't use racism as a reason why you  won't do your level  to make sure your child becomes a good  reader have you heard somebody do that  well there there are people who say you  know I don't believe in the system the  system is never gonna give me a shot  yeah I've gotten those kinds of calls on  radio people who are so fed up with or  who have given up and who won't engage  therefore and they say you know if it's  not gonna work for me  it probably won't work for my kid so  maybe I don't have the time or energy to  invest it there are folks who are sort  of really depressed probably bordering  on a clinical depression about sort of  the struggle to succeed if you're  growing up in the heart of the inner  city but we can't succumb to that the  slaves didn't succumb to it the Freedom  Riders didn't succumb to it  the people who were part of the civil  rights movement didn't succumb to it and  we can't succumb today because then  that's a recipe for chronic poverty  depression and isolation from the  mainstream and the point of the book is  there so many stories of resilient  people who know which end is up who are  just as poor who have mustered the  energy and the will to be successful and  that's what we almost did talk a lot  about reading as we have already named  several books three or four books that  you've always trying to be your favorite  well the most wonderful novel I've read  in the last 10 years is snow falling on  Cedars I just I just can't get over that  book I think it's a truly extraordinary  book I've started in Robert Caro's most  recent biography of Lyndon Johnson which  is quite remarkable I I vacillate  between sort of airplane reading very  light novels a lot of the stuff by James  Patterson I've gotten into Richard North  Patterson's works recently his book I  think it was a protect and defend I  think was a lot of fun in that in that  vein and then I then I go into fictional  biography Colin Powell's biography was  was very moving for me and to say that  the new biography and Lyndon Johnson the  biography of by David Reverend Lewis of  WB Dubois the two volumes extraordinary  histories a couple of others that are  read that over the years that I was very  moved by Kenneth man  wrote a book called black Apollo science  which is a story of earnest ever just  and Martin Duberman wrote a biography of  Paul Robeson and those two were  biographies were remarkable for me  because they they were the stories of of  men of outsized talent and ambition who  were confined by segregation they pushed  against the wall of segregation so hard  they ended up almost losing their  balance and both our Greek tragedies but  amazingly attractive brilliant men who  were ahead of their time who would be  just superstars in American society  today and the stories are so engaging a  matter of fact when I was in public  television heading up the division that  produces great performances we were so  interested in both of those books that  we tried to get the rights to do draw  international co-productions based on  them because there's such compelling  American stories so I I move between  fiction and nonfiction and very light  fiction how much how much do you read I  probably read a book every other week  depends on how much I'm flying if I fly  a lot then I'll consume more books right  now I've got them carrying around the  the biography of Frida Kahlo my wife and  I saw that new film absolutely loved it  and she and Diego Rivera and amazing  characters and I wanted to know more  about them so I bought the book it's  pretty thick and I'm cutting it around  with me you're there reference from time  to time about blacks and entertainment  and blacks and sports and then take  someone like yourself if you had us top  sports figure a top music figure and  Hugh price standing there which one do  you think the kids would go for as a  mentor well I think a couple years ago  obviously they'd gravitate toward the  athlete and the Glitter and and you know  probably some of the rock stars  I think after 9/11 there's been  interesting transformation and with the  way we think about heroism and I think  we've rediscovered everyday heroes and  the nobility of everyday life with the  celebration of the heroism of not just  the firefighters and the policemen but  the emergency workers who carried people  out the office workers who carried  people on the flight attendants on the  plain so I think something very healthy  has happened in that we have  rediscovered more accessible heroes  because we've got to let our young  people know that there's dignity in  everyday life in this heroism in  everyday life and I think that's that if  there's one good thing and it's probably  about the only good thing that's come up  9/11 it is it is that I mean you  actually see kids treating you  differently than they used to  yeah I mean we do these national  induction national achievers society  induction ceremonies that kids are out  there by the hundreds yeah they're  they're very interested in the real  world now much more rooted in real world  I mean the the the the the the hero I  worship the most among this celebrities  was Jackie Robinson when I was growing  up Jackie wasn't God he wasn't just a  superstar and I I was blessed to meet  him when I was nine years old and had my  picture taken with and I thought I was  having my picture taken with God because  of not only the physical accomplishments  but the the the bravery of the man  just an amazing man Janie Victoria Ward  you quote from author of the skin where  we're in teaching our children to be  emotionally strong socially smart and  spiritually connected what I'm getting  at is here she's suggesting that  visiting museums libraries attending  lectures and plays because the parents  feel those activities are too white yeah  what is that all about well I mean we've  just got to work on her parents about  that I think it's a little bit of a  function of time and not a whole lot of  exposure on the part of the parents but  in the book I talk about what can happen  when you visit a museum we took a bunch  of young people that I'm part of a group  that mentors the Westchester Club and  these black kids in elementary school up  in White Plains and all boys and we took  them to the Metropolitan Museum of Art  so that they could have a highbrow heart  experience and a couple of the kids were  into the the the paintings and such but  most of the boys weren't as we walk down  one of the corridors the kids spotted  the armor out of the corner of their eye  the testosterone  pump kicked on and they made a beeline  for the armor and there their noses were  smashed up against the glass trying to  read all the captions about the  crossbows and  muskets and all that all that hardware  had we had the presence of mind then and  what we certainly should do today is we  should have said you guys are in the  armor tomorrow let's go to the library  but let's go online and let's see what  you can discover about armor had they  done that think about they could have  learned a lot about the great religions  and the Crusades they could have learned  about the evolving geography of Europe  and how that's impacted the rest of the  world they could have learned some  science the metallurgy of how that stuff  is made they could learn some math I  mean how far would the shaft of a  crossbow  go if you pull it down against a certain  potential strength on the bow etc a lot  of the disciplines that are studied in  school could have been reinforced just  by that moment of exposure in a an  enrichment program I mean if you're deep  into baseball III as a kid used to I  thought I was gonna become a major  league baseball player and I was 15  before I realized it wasn't gonna happen  although all the pitchers who threw  curveballs that I couldn't hit and do it  over Friday but when I was in elementary  school I devoured baseball magazines and  I started out reading baseball magazines  for elementary school kids were pretty  soon I could read them for any age group  I learned that if you figure out how to  calculate a batting average in an earned  run average you've got long division  down cold but the point is you need to  look for where the light bulb goes off  where the spark appears and for the kids  who are in the museum with us it was the  armor and then all the standard stuff  that goes on in school could have been  reinforced by letting them explore their  newfound curiosity about this that can  happen in all sorts of environments  music the arts sports any number of  these places and I one of the things I  wish is that formal education were more  flexible so that when the light bulb  goes off they can develop a full  curricular and discovery experience  around it why do you think they're  interested armor what is it in the  society that draws them that well I mean  this is this I I wish I was enough of a  psychologist to say but there's that  there are things that boys are more  interested in than girls probably I hate  to say it I don't have a more  sophisticated understanding of  the net boys are probably more  interested in football than girls more  interested in certain kinds of contact  sports and girls I don't know but there  there was something about the the armor  and making war and all that kind of  stuff that that intrigued them and I  just know that they were drawn to it  you mentioned the West Chester the club  right what is it well there's a  wonderful group of african-american  professional men in West Chester called  the West Chester Clubman it was founded  some probably close to 40 years ago that  people had kept Callaway and Sidney  portait was in and it's doctors and  lawyers and others one of the things we  do is we each put up a substantial  amount of money a year to support an  after-school program for kids who are  struggling in school and that group is  called the club spawned by the West  Chester club men and we take those young  people and they they are in this program  every day after school for about three  hours they work on their homework we  then have enrichment programs for them  take them on trips we've taken them to  museums to the Wonderful's  Science Museum in New Jersey we've taken  them to West Point we've taken them to  auto shows I mean we've done all sorts  of things with them but the point is to  provide the kind of after-school support  that reinforces and supports both their  academic development in their social  development some of them have been taken  invited to visit us I had one of the  young people shadow me at the National  Urban League for a day they've saddled  some of the doctors in the organization  when I was at channel 13 we took a group  of the young people down to our studios  and to our editing Suites at channel 13  and it was great for them to see  african-americans women and men  everyplace in the studio they were on  camera they were behind the camera they  were in the editing Suites I mean one of  the greatest moments was when a  different of mine by the name of George  Miles who was executive vice president  station and is now the head of the  Pittsburgh public television station  George came out George about 63 and kind  of gruff sound and grew up in their  project looks a lot like Paul Robeson's  son actually had teased him about that  and George said to the kids he said when  I was growing or just a CPA he said when  I was growing up if anybody ever told me  I wasn't capable of doing something that  was  what I was gonna do and I was gonna  Shawn and he said they told me you  couldn't do this and do that and that  was exactly what I decided to show them  I could I could do and Excel it and to  see those kids react to that and to be  inspired where that was wonderful so we  try to expose them to people like George  and others who are extraordinarily  accomplished whose life circumstances in  the beginning were very similar and who  have climbed up and climbed to the top  and that's one of the beauties of a  well-conceived after-school program by  the way before we run out of time you're  leaving the Urban League in April what's  your next career I don't have anything  lined up there are sectors that I would  find potentially quite interesting I  think higher education is potentially  quite interested in the corporate world  the right portfolio could be interesting  something in philanthropy and maybe  media pundit REE I don't know I hope  something will present itself I have  pursued a couple of careers in my life  quite aggressively neither panned out in  both instances I think my lucky stars  within six months they didn't happen all  the situations I've been blessed to  pursue professionally have sort of  presented themselves and I trust my  instinct as to whether they're right for  me the Rockefeller Foundation you also  at the New York Times editorial board  public television public television of  all those which one did you really enjoy  the most I've enjoyed every one of them  I've never not enjoyed a career I can't  I can't do something professionally if I  don't want to get up in the morning and  do it and I go flat out when I do it so  they've they've all been fantastic  experiences they've all built on one  another my position that the National  Urban League is CEO has been has enabled  me to draw on all of those careers I  liken being the head of the National  Urban League to an applied liberal arts  course because everything you ever  learned and I ever learned in freshman  college freshman year at Amherst is in  play there so I've I've just had a  terrific time at everything I've ever  done if you had to name one thing that  you would remember as something he  learned at these places that start with  the New York Times editorial board what  would it be what you take away from  there you didn't know before well I  remember when I was getting sick of  writing the same editorial the same  theme same topic about ten times I went  in to see max Frankel who was the head  of the page at the time and I said I'm  really tired of this  they must have gotten the point by now  and Mac said what makes you think they  read it the first time what makes you  think they've absorbed the message what  makes you think they've changed their  behavior go back and write it you've got  to write it and write it and write it  until you're blue in the face  so that was a basic lesson from the  Rockefeller but from the New York Times  about imparting a message if you really  believe in it the Rockefeller Foundation  that was a marvelous experience because  we got to create things and to use the  Foundation's money to do that as I look  back on the National Guard Youth  Challenge Corps when I went in to see  the head of the National Guard in the  spring of 1989 and said you know the  military used to be a tremendous outlet  for kids who had lost their way and you  all stopped doing that  would you consider starting a domestic  youth corps for kids who dropped out of  school and the moment of Revelation was  when they said you know we think of  ourselves as youth workers we just train  him to make war but I didn't realize  that the military invest more on  understanding human development and then  the other institution on earth and that  conversation sparked it ignited their  interest in what came to be known as the  National Guard Youth Challenge Corps  which was started in the early 90s exist  to this day and has been tremendously  successful in getting dropouts back on  track what did you gain from or learn  from public television I'm just the  power of communications just the  absolute power of communications and the  power of reflective communications and  one of the things that I'm troubled by  is the superficiality of so much  coverage these days the entertainment  value that's been injected into the news  and and I have tremendous respect for  public television and public radio  because they they dare to take a longer  look at issues they dare to be more  reflective about the issues we can't  afford in a 21st century to get our news  from the news crawls we've got to crawl  behind the news and understand develop a  deeper understanding of what's at stake  and that has been the signature of  c-span it's the signature of public  television it's a signature of public  radio and I I think we've got to  preserve those values and then finally  the Urban League well I just I was I've  been blessed to work with  and to survey a great movement when I  was a young professional doing  consulting work I got to meet Whitney  Young Vernon Jordan John Jacob who my  predecessors and and I wanted to be like  them and I got to fulfill that dream I  got to serve a movement that's now  almost 93 years old that has helped  propel people into the American  mainstream professionally it doesn't get  much better than that fulfilling a dream  and being able to lead a great movement  on page 51 you have something called the  pledge of the national achievers Society  I want to read it I am excellent I am  excellent I am excellent my mind is a  pearl I can do anything anything that my  mind can conceive I can achieve anything  that my mind can conceive and my heart  can believe I can achieve I am excellent  I am excellent I am a national achiever  what's that all about  you'd have to be in a church in hearing  350 kids say then that's about a mindset  instilling and reinforcing a mindset  getting children to express their  commitment to their own excellence enter  their own possibilities we are trying to  spread that gospel of achievement  throughout our community and having  youngsters say that openly in front of  800 adults is very very affirming for  the kids it's very affirming for the  parents and it lays the foundation for  taking achievement truly seriously in  our entire community I mean I once said  to Mayor Mike Bloomberg that I think our  achiever society does fantastic thing  but I'd love to see a parade down Fifth  Avenue for all the kids who are doing  well in school or down Pennsylvania  Avenue in Washington or down Michigan  Avenue in Chicago and let the kids who  are doing well in school  march in that parade with their parents  on one hand and their teachers on the  other that's when we will really start  to impact the way our children see their  possibilities and the priorities that  they set for themselves can you remember  a program that you either had at the  Urban League or someplace else that you  decided this does not work and abandon  it well I think what we've done is by  trying to focus we have winnowed some  things I mean we're now about to get  more involved in help than we had  but we scaled back our health activities  for a while we were I I'd have to think  harder about that I don't know I don't  know one doesn't come instantly to mind  who walked around the block one of my  colleagues at the National Urban League  a mother who's who lives in Philadelphia  and she's got a grueling commute from  Philly to New York and back every day  and when she gets home she walks around  the block with her child and she knows  that her child says let's walk around  again or if she's really tired doesn't  want to go around the block and the  daughter says mom we need to take a walk  there's something that John wants to  talk about it's a very very powerful  message very interesting do you do  anything as a mentor that you know  always works you can see it kids change  I think when you lock in on a kid and  let them know you really are interested  in them that you're really listening to  them and you want to help them unlock  their own potential that's that's  incredibly gratifying whether it's with  your own children or with a young person  that you're mentoring and and in the  book I talked about a youngster who was  sort of half asleep and the in the club  program one day and we came in and and I  asked what he'd been doing in school and  he muttered that they were supposed to  do something about writing editorials  and I said that you know I used to be an  editorial writer and so one eye popped  open and I asked him how do you go about  writing editorials and he sort of came  up to a 25 degree angle and before we  were done he was sitting bolt upright  and we were fully engaged and this was a  kid who looked at me as a suit and a tie  and it was obviously dialed out but by  the time we were done that day we were  locked in his mother came up to me in a  Barnes &amp; Noble book store about a year  ago and said do you remember him his  name was definite just I do you remember  him I said yeah very vividly said  Devin's just about to finish his junior  year in college and he's studying to be  a physical therapist this is a kid who  was sort of half asleep disconnected  could care less so when you see that and  and you've made that connect and can  look back on  the time when you may have ignited a  spark it's very exciting when did you  take away from the book to her the  probably the fundamental message was the  question I kept being asked by parents  that come to the bookstores in the  african-american community it's all I  can do to provide food clothing and  shelter why can't why can't I just trust  the teachers and the message of the book  is that you can't just trust the  teachers ronald reagan's politics were  never my politics but he said of nuclear  disarmament who must trust but verify in  other words there are matters of  enormous importance you must trust but  verify so it reinforced the fact that  I've got to keep explaining to folks why  I know how busy you are but you've got  to get engaged for all the reasons that  we talked about in the book you've got  to monitor what's going on you got to be  sure that what the teachers are doing is  in the best interest of your child and  you've got to let the schools know and  the kids know that you're on the case  you can't just trust you must trust but  verify this is the cover of the book  it's called achievement matters by Hubie  price the outgoing president of the  National Urban League we thank you very  much thank you my pleasure
 

